#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176918} :activity/id #uuid "4afc24dd-0109-5622-989e-7e2ca31e311d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049023 :topic/id #uuid "d48953c1-0284-552d-8c62-2e8502548973" :topic/math-academy-id 3577} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175949 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175797 :question/id #uuid "1c852599-e08e-5b54-be2b-700c3f8f47f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6931"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500345}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term"} :step/id #uuid "a3a92aca-9db7-5c85-bff4-92c8a5b8ea08" :step/math-academy-id 25611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176064 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175725 :question/id #uuid "2ed3f2e8-2d0e-59e0-a9dc-1856245b768c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41472e46-65d2-579b-85b7-309749ed3a5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050526}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500320}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"} :step/id #uuid "1445b874-9aa3-5b17-b1f5-d21b64581d54" :step/math-academy-id 4262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176965 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7336a5d6-0b38-5ba4-b278-aaac6a402ece" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ed6467d-dfe0-510d-a23b-700569abb8d5" :step/math-academy-id 5062 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176883}}] :activity/title "Factoring Trinomials Using Common Factors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176126}]}
